\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Chumashan languages (ISO 639-3 chs) have no first-language speakers today; tribal members and activists pursue revitalization. Barbare\~no and Inese\~no (Samala) are the best documented varieties, largely through J.\ P.\ Harrington's unpublished fieldnotes. This report is short: only encyclopedic and reference pages were found in search, and no school curriculum was located.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school curricula were found in the search results. Programmes mentioned by the sources (to be verified directly): the Santa Ynez Band of Chumash Indians Language Program (Samala), the Barbare\~no Chumash Council, and the \v{S}muwi\v{c} Language School at Wishtoyo Chumash Village in Malibu (opened 2010 according to the sources).

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Overview of the Chumashan languages: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chumashan_languages}
\item Barbare\~no language overview, including history of revival and references: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbare%C3%B1o_language}
\item Omniglot page on Barbare\~no: \url{https://omniglot.com/writing/barbareno.htm}
\item Berkeley Language Center page on Samala (Inese\~no): \url{https://cla.berkeley.edu/languages/ineseno.html}
\end{itemize}
Sources cite Applegate's 1972 Inese\~no grammar dissertation and a 2008 grammar and dictionary; their locations were not confirmed by search.

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found beyond the community organizations named above.

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
A ``Samala Chumash Language Tutorial'' site is cited in the reference lists, but no working URL was seen in results, so it is not listed.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
A K-12 curriculum would need to be built in partnership with the tribal language programmes, from archival (Harrington) sources. The content available through search is limited.

\end{document}
